Z||000027||Pontiaddict||10-15-2000||01:56 AM||||Here's an interesting tidbit of info.<P>The same guy that led the design team for the Aztek (I really don't think it's ugly) Is designing the next generation Corvette.<P>Dodge is saying that the PT Cruiser is patterned after the Chrysler (DeSoto) Airflow. Well when the Airflow, named because it claimed to be the first car to be designed using windtunnel testing, was a sales flop! Nobody liked the odd shape of the car. So after about 60 years, they build a copy of it and people love it. (why I don't know) they also use the fake nostalga to sell it. <P>So prepare yourself for the 2044 Pontiac Supercar (Now that's a name!)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000028||Phil||10-15-2000||05:16 PM||||Compare a PT Curiser to a '37 Ford Wagon and you'll see where the design came from.||||reg||1
Z||000029||lou_dias||10-15-2000||10:18 PM||||I'm not going to bother comparing the old bug vs. the new bug.  Bottom line is that it was a sales success because the car is practical to the people that want to own it.  The same must apply to a new Fiero.  They need to build a basic Fiero that is the sporty commuter for $15 and then need to build the true sports car version with engine and suspension and interior as well as some exterior styling  for $23-25k and a couple of models inbetween.  It can be done and done profittably.  Look at what you get in a F-body for $28k...||||reg||1
